produces a tender erythematous oedematous plaque, often initially misdiagnosed and treated as an infection (erysipelas or cellulitis), but antibiotics are ineffective. ·Extramammary Paget disease (EMPD)—a slow-growing, itchy, erythematous plaque in anogenital skin, especially the vulva. Biopsy shows mucin-containing tumour cells within the epidermis. EMPD is usually associated with an underlying adnexal carcinoma, often of apocrine origin, which may be difficult to localize, but $10-15\%$ of patients have cancer of the rectum, prostate, bladder, cervix, or urethra.
